Suppose $NN$ were bounded above and let $c = sup NN$. Then $c - 1$ is not an upper bound, so some $n in NN$ satisfies $n > c - 1$; but then $n + 1 in NN$ and $n + 1 > c$, contradicting that $c$ is an upper bound. Consequently, for every real $t > 0$ some $n in NN$ exceeds $1 slash t$, giving $(1)/(n) < t$; in particular $inf{(1)/(n) : n in NN} = 0$, and there are no infinitely large or infinitely small real numbers.
